    "solution": "At equilibrium the spring force is as large as the gravitational force. We set up Hooke's law and solve for *k*:\n\n$$ F = k \\cdot \\Delta l \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad k = \\frac{F}{\\Delta l} $$\n\nThe force pulling on the spring is the gravitational force $F = m \\cdot g$.\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nm = 320\\ \\mathrm{g} = 0.320\\ \\mathrm{kg} \\\\\ng \\approx 9.82\\ \\mathrm{N/kg} \\\\\n\\Delta l = 6.5\\ \\mathrm{cm} = 0.065\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ F = 0.320 \\cdot 9.82 = 3.1424\\ \\mathrm{N} $$\n\n$$ k = \\frac{3.1424}{0.065} = 48.3\\ \\mathrm{N/m} $$\n\n**Answer:** The spring constant is about $48\\ \\mathrm{N/m}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** Two formulas in a chain: first $F = mg$ gives the gravitational force, then $k = F/\\Delta l$ gives the spring constant."
