    "solution": "At the moment of release the weight is at its lower turning point ($y = -0.10\\ \\mathrm{m}$). The resultant force from the spring (in addition to the gravitational force, which is already balanced in the equilibrium position) is\n\n$$ F_R = -k \\cdot y $$\n\nThe acceleration follows from Newton's second law:\n\n$$ F_R = m \\cdot a \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a = \\frac{F_R}{m} = \\frac{-k \\cdot y}{m} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nk = 25\\ \\mathrm{N/m} \\\\\ny = -10\\ \\mathrm{cm} = -0.10\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\nm = 0.40\\ \\mathrm{kg}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ a = \\frac{-25 \\cdot (-0.10)}{0.40} = \\frac{2.5}{0.40} = 6.25\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2} $$\n\n**Answer:** The acceleration is about $6.3\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$, directed upwards towards the equilibrium position.\n\n**General conclusion:** In the formula $F_R = -k\\cdot y$ the gravitational force is already accounted for in the equilibrium position: we only count the spring's *extra* force beyond what balances the weight. That is why the acceleration at the lower turning point is *not* $g$ but depends on *k* and the displacement."
