    "solution": "The potential energy at a turning point is the total energy of the whole oscillation:\n\n$$ E = \\frac{k \\cdot A^2}{2} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nk = 120\\ \\mathrm{N/m} \\\\\nA = 15\\ \\mathrm{cm} = 0.15\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ E = \\frac{120 \\cdot 0.15^2}{2} = \\frac{120 \\cdot 0.0225}{2} = 1.35\\ \\mathrm{J} $$\n\n**Answer:** The energy is $1.35\\ \\mathrm{J}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** Stored energy grows with the *square* of the displacement: stretching twice as far gives four times as much energy."
