    "solution": "At the moment of release (the turning point) all the energy is potential. When passing through the equilibrium position all the energy has been converted into kinetic energy.\n\n**The principle of conservation of energy:**\n\n$$ \\frac{k \\cdot A^2}{2} = \\frac{m \\cdot v^2}{2} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad v = A\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nk = 48\\ \\mathrm{N/m} \\\\\nA = 12\\ \\mathrm{cm} = 0.12\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\nm = 0.30\\ \\mathrm{kg}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ v = 0.12 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{0.30}} = 0.12 \\cdot \\sqrt{160} = 0.12 \\cdot 12.649 \\approx 1.52\\ \\mathrm{m/s} $$\n\n**Answer:** The weight has a speed of about $1.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ when it passes the equilibrium position.\n\n**General conclusion:** Energy alternates between potential form (at the turning points) and kinetic form (at the equilibrium position). The sum is constant as long as no energy is lost."
