    "solution": "We first calculate the angular velocity and then the period.\n\n$$ \\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}, \\qquad T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nk = 64\\ \\mathrm{N/m} \\\\\nm = 0.25\\ \\mathrm{kg}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ \\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{0.25}} = \\sqrt{256} = 16\\ \\mathrm{rad/s} $$\n\n$$ T = \\frac{2\\pi}{16} = 0.3927\\ldots\\ \\mathrm{s} \\approx 0.39\\ \\mathrm{s} $$\n\n**Answer:** The period is about $0.39\\ \\mathrm{s}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** The period of a mass on a spring depends only on the spring constant and the mass, not on the amplitude. A large amplitude does not give a longer period."
