    "solution": "The velocity is the derivative of the position with respect to time:\n\n$$ v = y' = \\omega \\cdot A\\cos(\\omega \\cdot t) $$\n\nComparing with the standard form $y = A\\sin(\\omega t)$ gives $A = 0.12\\ \\mathrm{m}$ and $\\omega = 8.0\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}$.\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nA = 0.12\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\n\\omega = 8.0\\ \\mathrm{rad/s} \\\\\nt = 0.10\\ \\mathrm{s}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ v = 8.0 \\cdot 0.12 \\cos(8.0 \\cdot 0.10) = 0.96\\cos(0.80) $$\n\nWith the calculator in **radian mode**: $\\cos(0.80) \\approx 0.6967$, so\n\n$$ v \\approx 0.96 \\cdot 0.6967 \\approx 0.67\\ \\mathrm{m/s} $$\n\n**Answer:** The speed is about $0.67\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** Do not forget that the calculator must be in radian mode when the argument is $\\omega t$. The maximum speed is $v_{\\max} = \\omega A = 0.96\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$, and our speed of $0.67\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ lies reasonably a little below that."
