    "solution": "We first need the spring constant *k* from Hooke's law, and then the period with the new mass.\n\n**Step 1: the spring constant.** The gravitational force on the first weight gives the extension $\\Delta l$:\n\n$$ k = \\frac{m_1 \\cdot g}{\\Delta l} $$\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nm_1 = 200\\ \\mathrm{g} = 0.200\\ \\mathrm{kg} \\\\\ng \\approx 9.82\\ \\mathrm{N/kg} \\\\\n\\Delta l = 5.0\\ \\mathrm{cm} = 0.050\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ k = \\frac{0.200 \\cdot 9.82}{0.050} = 39.28\\ \\mathrm{N/m} $$\n\n**Step 2: the period with the new mass.**\n\n$$ T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m_2}{k}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.500}{39.28}} = 2\\pi\\cdot 0.1129 \\approx 0.71\\ \\mathrm{s} $$\n\n**Answer:** The period is about $0.71\\ \\mathrm{s}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** Two formulas in a chain: Hooke's law gives *k* from the extension caused by the first weight, then the period formula gives *T* from the new mass. Note that the amplitude plays no role."
