    "solution": "**Step 1: read off the wavelength from the diagram.** The wavelength is the distance between two neighbouring crests (or two neighbouring zero crossings in the same direction). The diagram shows $\\lambda = 0.50\\ \\mathrm{m}$.\n\n**Step 2: calculate the frequency.**\n\n$$ f = \\frac{v}{\\lambda} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nv = 6.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s} \\\\\n\\lambda = 0.50\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ f = \\frac{6.0}{0.50} = 12\\ \\mathrm{Hz} $$\n\n**Answer:** The frequency is $12\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** A $y$-$x$ diagram (the wave at one instant along a string) shows the wavelength directly; a $y$-$t$ diagram (the position of one point against time) shows the period. Both are used to calculate the speed via $v = f\\cdot\\lambda$."
