    "solution": "**The key insight is that the angle of refraction *b* cannot exceed $90^\\circ$**: at $b = 90^\\circ$ the refracted wave would run **parallel** to the surface, which is the limit. At a larger angle of incidence no refraction can take place, and everything is reflected back (*total reflection*).\n\n**Condition for the critical angle:**\n\n$$ \\frac{\\sin i_\\text{critical}}{\\sin 90^\\circ} = \\frac{v_1}{v_2} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\sin i_\\text{critical} = \\frac{v_1}{v_2} $$\n\nGiven values:\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nv_1 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s} \\\\\nv_2 = 8.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ \\sin i_\\text{critical} = \\frac{3.0}{8.0} = 0.375 $$\n\n$$ i_\\text{critical} = \\sin^{-1}(0.375) \\approx 22.0^\\circ $$\n\n**Answer:** Total reflection occurs at angles of incidence greater than about $22^\\circ$.\n\n**General conclusion:** Total reflection can **only** happen when the wave goes from a *slow* to a *fast* medium (here shallow → deep water). There is then a critical angle beyond which $\\sin b$ would become $> 1$, which is impossible. The same principle is used in optical fibres: light going from glass towards air is totally reflected and kept inside the fibre."
