    "solution": "For **constructive interference** (antinodal lines), $\\Delta s = n\\cdot\\lambda$. The point has $\\Delta s = 2\\lambda$, so $n = 2$: it is the **2nd antinodal line**.\n\nFor the nodal lines (destructive interference) $\\Delta s$ would be an odd number of half wavelengths: $\\lambda/2, 3\\lambda/2, 5\\lambda/2, \\ldots$.\n\n**General conclusion:** The central maximum ($n = 0$, $\\Delta s = 0$) lies where the distances to both sources are equal. Then follow the 0th nodal line ($\\lambda/2$), the 1st antinodal line ($\\lambda$), the 1st nodal line ($3\\lambda/2$), the 2nd antinodal line ($2\\lambda$), and so on."
