    "correct": 3,
    "solution": "**Diffraction** is the bending of waves at edges or narrow openings: the phenomenon that a wave \"bends around corners\" or spreads out after a slit. **Interference** is the superposition of waves: when waves from different sources (or different parts of the same wave that has spread out) meet, they can reinforce each other (antinodal lines) or cancel each other (nodal lines).\n\nIn the double-slit experiment both work together: first the wave diffracts through each slit, and then the two diffracted waves interfere with each other.\n\n**General conclusion:** Both phenomena are characteristic of **waves**. They reveal that something is a wave: for example, double-slit experiments showed in 1801 that light is a wave motion, and in 1927 that electrons are waves too."
